  "title": "Noun endings: singular",
  "description": "Typical singular endings for masculine, feminine and neuter nouns in every case.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "These are typical patterns; some nouns differ (for example, many masculine nouns take -a instead of -u in the genitive). Masculine nouns for people and animals use the genitive form in the accusative: widzę kota, but widzę sklep.",
      "table": {
        "columns": [
          "Case",
          "sklep, shop (m, thing)",
          "kot, cat (m, animal)",
          "kobieta, woman (f)",
          "okno, window (n)"
        ],
        "rows": [
          ["Nominative", "sklep", "kot", "kobieta", "okno"],
          ["Genitive", "sklepu", "kota", "kobiety", "okna"],
          ["Dative", "sklepowi", "kotu", "kobiecie", "oknu"],
          ["Accusative", "sklep", "kota", "kobietę", "okno"],
          ["Instrumental", "sklepem", "kotem", "kobietą", "oknem"],
          ["Locative", "sklepie", "kocie", "kobiecie", "oknie"],
          ["Vocative", "sklepie", "kocie", "kobieto", "okno"]
        ]
      }
